WEBVTT

1
00:00:07.007 --> 00:00:08.188
Animals are amazing!

2
00:00:10.229 --> 00:00:12.190
Some animals can breathe underwater.

3
00:00:15.051 --> 00:00:18.193
Some have protective skeletons on the outside of their bodies.

4
00:00:20.314 --> 00:00:22.675
A few can even regrow lost limbs!

5
00:00:24.596 --> 00:00:27.078
Animals can do these things thanks to their bodies.

6
00:00:29.299 --> 00:00:33.469
Humans can't do any of these things, but wouldn't it be great if we could?

7
00:00:35.809 --> 00:00:39.790
Imagining humans with these features brings up some incredible questions.

8
00:00:41.451 --> 00:00:45.792
Fish can live underwater because they have gills that take oxygen from water.

9
00:00:47.892 --> 00:00:49.413
What if humans had gills?

10
00:00:51.754 --> 00:00:54.994
We could swim all day without having to surface for a breath.

11
00:00:56.235 --> 00:00:58.095
Why haven't humans evolved gills?

12
00:00:58.995 --> 00:01:00.556
It would be so convenient!

13
00:01:01.316 --> 00:01:02.417
Or maybe not.

14
00:01:05.139 --> 00:01:07.841
All mammals, including humans, are warm-blooded.

15
00:01:08.722 --> 00:01:13.726
This means they produce their own heat to keep themselves warm and regulate their body temperature.

16
00:01:14.406 --> 00:01:15.967
This takes a lot of oxygen.

17
00:01:17.308 --> 00:01:18.930
Fish don't need much oxygen.

18
00:01:19.630 --> 00:01:20.931
They are cold-blooded.

19
00:01:21.632 --> 00:01:25.034
Their body temperature changes depending on the surrounding temperature.

20
00:01:27.688 --> 00:01:33.632
A human's lungs are much larger than a fish's gills because humans need more oxygen to create body

21
00:01:33.672 --> 00:01:36.014
heat. Gills aren't meant for this.

22
00:01:37.575 --> 00:01:42.679
If humans had gills large enough to provide the oxygen we need, they would be enormous.

23
00:01:43.559 --> 00:01:45.541
Our bodies would look completely different!

24
00:01:47.842 --> 00:01:50.684
Gills are better for smaller, cold-blooded creatures.

25
00:01:53.026 --> 00:01:55.688
Another cool animal feature is the exoskeleton.

26
00:01:56.584 --> 00:01:58.966
That's a skeleton on the outside of the body.

27
00:01:59.726 --> 00:02:02.488
Insects, spiders, and crustaceans have them.

28
00:02:03.709 --> 00:02:05.310
It's not a rare feature at all.

29
00:02:05.930 --> 00:02:09.712
More than 80 percent of all animals on earth have exoskeletons.

30
00:02:10.453 --> 00:02:11.453
So why not humans?

31
00:02:13.915 --> 00:02:16.156
Built-in armor would certainly look cool!

32
00:02:17.217 --> 00:02:21.580
But it might not work as well for humans as it does for tarantulas or lobsters.

33
00:02:22.808 --> 00:02:26.509
Like gills, exoskeletons are best suited to small creatures.

34
00:02:27.309 --> 00:02:30.390
An exoskeleton has to be light enough for a creature to carry.

35
00:02:31.351 --> 00:02:33.551
And it has to provide the creature with protection.

36
00:02:35.732 --> 00:02:38.653
A human, of course, is much larger than a shrimp.

37
00:02:39.373 --> 00:02:43.354
Even a thin exoskeleton would be far too heavy for us to carry around.

38
00:02:44.955 --> 00:02:49.256
This is one of the challenges the military faces when designing exoskeleton armor.

39
00:02:50.204 --> 00:02:54.026
It's hard to find materials that are super light but also strong.

40
00:02:55.666 --> 00:02:58.888
An exoskeleton might not even offer a human much protection.

41
00:03:01.029 --> 00:03:04.310
Animals that have exoskeletons go through a process called molting.

42
00:03:05.090 --> 00:03:08.152
This is when they shed their old skeletons to grow new ones.

43
00:03:09.532 --> 00:03:12.413
During molting, an animal is left with no protection.

44
00:03:14.594 --> 00:03:18.136
Luckily their new, small exoskeletons grow pretty quickly.

45
00:03:20.128 --> 00:03:23.952
But it would take a human much longer to grow a new skeleton than it would take a

46
00:03:24.012 --> 00:03:24.712
small crab.

47
00:03:26.113 --> 00:03:29.837
All that time the person would be in danger—and unable to stand up.

48
00:03:31.438 --> 00:03:35.502
After all, during molting, we wouldn't have a skeleton to support our muscles.

49
00:03:37.724 --> 00:03:41.367
Not all animal kingdom adaptations would be impractical for humans.

50
00:03:42.668 --> 00:03:46.932
Some animals, like starfish, have the ability to regenerate lost limbs.

51
00:03:48.212 --> 00:03:52.236
This really would be useful since losing a limb can be a major challenge.

52
00:03:54.117 --> 00:03:57.901
Humans can already grow new blood cells, skin, and muscle.

53
00:03:58.621 --> 00:04:01.444
But no mammal can regrow an entire lost limb.

54
00:04:03.646 --> 00:04:05.247
Scientists aren't sure why.

55
00:04:06.668 --> 00:04:08.450
It may have something to do with scars.

56
00:04:10.512 --> 00:04:14.896
When a body part is injured, the body produces chemicals that help prevent infection.

57
00:04:15.540 --> 00:04:17.701
Those chemicals also cause scars.

58
00:04:18.942 --> 00:04:22.164
Starfish don't get scars—they just grow new limbs.

59
00:04:24.545 --> 00:04:27.967
But human cells are programmed to only grow in a controlled way.

60
00:04:28.688 --> 00:04:32.470
Cancer is a disease that occurs from cells that grow uncontrollably.

61
00:04:34.411 --> 00:04:37.773
Scientists don't think regrowing limbs is completely impossible.

62
00:04:38.333 --> 00:04:42.195
In fact, they are making progress in learning to grow human body parts.

63
00:04:43.036 --> 00:04:46.359
With luck, this animal what-if might one day be real.

64
00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:50.963
Animals can do many things that humans can't do.

65
00:04:52.905 --> 00:04:56.468
But our bodies let us do some incredible things that animals can't do.

66
00:04:57.309 --> 00:05:02.233
Because of the structure of our bodies, we can talk and communicate in ways that animals can't.

67
00:05:05.296 --> 00:05:08.259
We're the only animal that walks upright all the time.

68
00:05:10.672 --> 00:05:13.753
Our hands are more flexible and useful than other animals' hands.

69
00:05:16.255 --> 00:05:20.036
Thanks to the complexity of our brains, we can figure out how cells work.

70
00:05:22.777 --> 00:05:25.339
And best of all, we can imagine what-ifs!

71
00:05:26.519 --> 00:05:28.340
Animals definitely can't do that!
